course: biology
lesson: 210
title: Pigs: mud, snouts and brains
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: People call a messy room a pigsty, but pigs are among the cleanest and cleverest animals on the farm.
explain: A pig rolls in the mud for the same reason you put on sun cream and jump in a pool on a hot day: pigs can hardly sweat, so a coat of wet mud cools them down and stops their thin, pale skin from burning.
explain: Given space, pigs keep their toilet far away from where they sleep, and their strong, sensitive snout digs up roots and grubs like a living spade.
explain: Pigs learn names, solve puzzles and in one study even steered a joystick with their snouts to play a simple computer game.
fact: Pigs wallow in mud to keep cool, because they cannot sweat much, and the mud also protects their skin from sunburn.
fact: Pigs are clean when they have space: they keep their toilet area well away from their beds.
fact: A pig's snout is strong and very sensitive, used for rooting up roots and grubs, and pigs have been used to find truffles, a mushroom that grows underground.
fact: Pigs are omnivores, eaters of both plants and animals, that eat roots, fruit, grain, insects and more; in many places it is banned to feed them kitchen scraps with meat, to stop diseases spreading.
fact: Pigs are among the most intelligent farm animals: they solve puzzles, learn names, and in a study reported in 2021 pigs used a joystick with their snouts to play a simple computer game.
fact: A sow, a mother pig, can have a litter of a dozen piglets or more born together; each piglet picks a teat to suck milk from and keeps it, and piglets know their mother's voice.
fact: A frightened pig squeals very loudly to call for help, and close up a squeal was reported to be about as loud as a rock concert.
fact: Many pigs are pink, but many breeds are black, spotted, ginger or hairy, and their wild ancestor, the wild boar, is hairy and brown.
fact: Pale pigs can get sunburnt, because their skin is thin with little hair, so mud and shade protect them, a bit like sun cream and a hat protect us.
fact: «Eating like a pig» is unfair: pigs that always have enough food usually stop when they are full.
fact: A pig often curls and wags its tail when happy, while a tail hanging straight down can mean it is unwell or stressed; pigs use more than twenty different grunts and squeals.
fact: «Mini pigs» sold as pets often grow much bigger than promised, to the size of a big dog or more, and many end up in rescue centres.
exercise: Why does a pig roll in the mud? | To keep cool and protect its skin from the sun | Because it likes being dirty and smelly more than any other animal | To hide from the farmer | To clean its teeth
exercise: Why can't a pig cool down by sweating like us? | Pigs can hardly sweat | Pigs sweat only from their ears | Pigs sweat too much and get cold | Pigs sweat only in winter
exercise: Are pigs dirty animals when they have enough space? | No, they keep their toilet far from their beds | Yes, they sleep in their own toilet and like the smell | Yes, they never move from one spot | No, because they never go to the toilet
exercise: What is a pig doing when it digs with its snout? | Rooting for food such as roots and grubs | Building a nest for the eggs it is going to lay in spring | Looking for water to drink | Sharpening its nose
exercise: What have pigs been used to find? | Truffles, mushrooms that grow underground | Gold coins buried long ago by pirates and robbers | Lost sheep | Water pipes
exercise: What kind of eater is a pig? | An omnivore that eats plants and animals | A plant eater only | A meat eater only, like a wolf or a lion | An eater of grass only, like a cow
exercise: Why is feeding pigs kitchen scraps with meat banned in many places? | To stop diseases spreading | Because pigs hate the taste of meat | Because it makes pigs too clever | Because it turns pigs bright pink
exercise: What did pigs do in a computer game study? | Moved a joystick with their snouts | Typed words with their hooves | Watched cartoons all day | Played chess against a dog
exercise: How big can a litter of piglets be? | A dozen or more | Always just one piglet | Exactly two, like twins | About a hundred piglets
exercise: How do piglets share their mother's milk? | Each piglet picks its own teat and keeps it | They take turns every hour | The biggest piglet drinks it all and the rest have to wait | The farmer feeds them from bottles only
exercise: Why does a young pig squeal so loudly when it is grabbed? | It is calling for help, like any prey animal | It is singing to its mother so that she knows where it is | It is laughing | It wants more food
exercise: What did the wild ancestor of pigs look like? | A hairy brown wild boar | A pink pig with no hair at all | A small spotted forest deer | A hippo with a curly tail
exercise: Why can pale pigs get sunburnt? | Their skin is thin with little hair | They stay up too late | Their mud is too cold | They eat too many carrots, which makes their skin turn pink
exercise: Does a pig with plenty of food usually stuff itself until it is ill? | No, it usually stops when it is full | Yes, pigs never stop eating while there is food | Yes, pigs keep eating until they burst | No, pigs eat only once a week, on Sundays
exercise: What can a pig's tail hanging straight down mean? | It may be unwell or stressed | It is very happy | It is about to fall asleep | It has just eaten
exercise: How many different grunts and squeals do pigs use? | More than twenty | Only one sound, a grunt | Exactly three different ones | None, pigs are silent
exercise: What often happens with a pet «mini pig»? | It grows much bigger than promised | It stays the size of a cat forever | It turns into a wild boar | It shrinks as it gets older
test: «Peppa Pig is pink so all pigs are pink, obvs» What is right? | Many pigs are pink, but many are black, spotted, ginger or hairy | All pigs are pink, without exception, everywhere in the whole world | Pigs are only pink in summer | Only cartoon pigs have colours
test: In a cartoon a pig lives happily in a filthy sty, rolling in its own mess. What is true of real pigs with space? | They keep their toilet far away from where they sleep | They love sleeping in their own mess because it keeps them warm | They never get dirty at all | They take baths in milk
test: «sow had 11 piglets, sorry 12. how many tails with mum» | 13 | 12 | 11 | 24
test: «4 pigs and a farmer in the yard, how many legs. no elephants so no trunks lol» | 18 | 16 | 20 | 22
test: What would happen if pale pigs had no mud or shade on a hot sunny day? | They could overheat and get sunburnt | They would turn black | They would sweat and stay cool | Nothing, pigs love strong sun
test: «you eat like a pig» — is that fair to pigs? | No, pigs with enough food usually stop when they are full | Yes, pigs eat until they burst | Yes, pigs eat all day long without a break, from morning until night | No, pigs never eat at all
test: «my mate's getting a mini pig, it'll stay tiny like a puppy» What should he know? | Mini pigs often grow as big as a big dog or more | Mini pigs never grow at all, they stay the size of a puppy for life | Mini pigs turn into rabbits | Mini pigs are born full size
test: «shut that pig up, it's screaming like mad just cos I lifted it» Why so loud? | It is frightened and calling for help, as prey animals do | It is singing loudly because it is happy to be picked up and cuddled | Pigs scream every time they eat | It is trying to sound like a dog
test: «pigs r thick though» Which fact shows they are clever? | They learn names, solve puzzles and used a joystick in a study | They can read and write | They can talk in words | They forget everything after a minute, but they can learn to sit like a dog
test: «pig's tail's all curly and wagging» What does that usually mean? | The pig is probably happy | The pig is very ill | The pig is angry | The pig is cold
test: A pig on a farm is pushing its nose into the soil and turning it over. What is it doing? | Rooting for roots and grubs to eat | Burying its piglets | Looking for its lost tail | Trying to escape under the fence
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of wallowing in mud among pigs? | Mud serves to cool an animal largely incapable of sweating and to protect its skin from the sun | Wallowing is undertaken because pigs prefer filth to any clean place in which to rest | Wallowing serves solely to conceal the animal from predators | Mud is consumed as the principal food of the pig
exam: Identify the principal function of the snout of the domestic pig. | Rooting in the soil for roots and grubs, with great sensitivity of smell and touch | Producing the animal's characteristic squeal | Regulating the body temperature by sweating through the nostrils on hot days | Serving as an organ for climbing trees
exam: It is given that a so-called mini pig is acquired as a household pet. Ascertain the outcome most frequently reported. | The animal grows considerably larger than promised, frequently to the size of a large dog | The animal remains permanently the size of a kitten, exactly as the breeders invariably promise | The animal ceases growing upon its arrival | The animal diminishes in size with age
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouration of domestic pigs and their ancestry. | Pigs may be pink, black, spotted, ginger or hairy, and descend from the hairy brown wild boar | All pigs are pink, as were their ancestors | Pigs descend from the hippopotamus | Pigs are invariably black in the wild state
